from pathlib import Path
p=Path('outputs/Architecture-and-Pilot.md');s=p.read_text(encoding='utf-8-sig')
s=s.replace('Employees freely choose whether to accept available paid delivery assignments.','The store manager decides whether an employee can be spared for delivery duty. Released employees freely choose whether to accept available paid delivery assignments.')
s=s.replace('Managers should check staff coverage, paid hours, vehicle approval and capacity before offering.','The manager must release an employee for delivery duty after checking store coverage, paid hours, vehicle approval and capacity. The server blocks employee acceptance and departure without that release. The manager can recall the employee, blocking new acceptance and departure while allowing an already-departed delivery to finish safely.')
s=s.replace('An interested employee can offer','An interested employee, subject to manager release for delivery duty, can offer')
s=s.replace('The employee who adds the route chooses whether to accept deliveries on that corridor, with no penalty for declining.','The employee who adds the route chooses whether to accept deliveries on that corridor, with no penalty for declining. The manager retains the final staffing decision: no employee can accept or depart until the manager releases them for delivery duty. A route entry is a coverage exception, not staffing permission.')
s=s.replace('Entities: orders, assistance applications, fund balance, rewards and audit events.','Entities: orders, assistance applications, employee-added coverage routes, manager duty releases, fund balance, rewards and audit events.')
s=s.replace('## Source basis and required professional review','''## Long-term drone hub roadmap

The long-term ambition is a delivery network capable of serving eligible grocery deliveries by drone from central hubs, with Abingdon as an illustrative candidate for evaluation. No actual site, operator, flight path, Food City agreement or approval is claimed. The goal of ultimately supporting all deliveries by drone is a design ambition, not an established payload, coverage or economic capability. Ground service remains the fallback wherever weather, weight, access, terrain or approved operating limits prevent a flight.

Keep the customer order and eligibility workflow independent of transport mode. Proposed additional entities: Hub, FulfillmentTask, CarrierAssignment, Package (weight, dimensions, cold-chain class), DroneMission, AirspaceAuthorization and DeliveryEvidence. One order can split across packages. A transport adapter exposes quote, capacity check, reserve, dispatch, track, cancel and confirm. Employee assignments use the ground adapter, while a future certified partner provides a drone adapter. Never translate a customer checkout directly into an aircraft command.

A drone dispatch proposal would pass through hub preparation, package/weight validation, a customer-approved delivery point, weather and operating-envelope checks, operator authorization, mission acceptance, launch, in-flight monitoring and delivery confirmation. The flight operator retains operational control. Callback signatures, idempotent mission updates, lost-link and aborted-flight handling, ground fallback and cold-chain reconciliation need dedicated engineering. Manager staffing release still controls store employees preparing or performing ground legs. Employee-added ground-route exceptions do not authorize airspace or drone coverage.

Readiness sequence: (1) evaluate geography and payload mix with a qualified operator, including an Abingdon hub feasibility study; (2) simulate missions using synthetic orders; (3) assess site, community, privacy, environmental and aviation requirements; (4) obtain applicable operator/operation approvals and conduct a limited authorized demonstration; (5) measure delivery success, cost, noise, weather downtime, payload exclusions and ground fallback before expansion. Drone expenditure is outside the one-store employee-delivery budget. Any drone business case needs separate capital, operator, insurance, maintenance, battery, hub labor and failure-cost estimates.

FAA guidance describes Part 135 certification and applicable approvals for compensated package delivery beyond visual line of sight. Confirm the requirements in effect at deployment with the operator and aviation counsel. Source: [FAA Package Delivery by Drone](https://www.faa.gov/uas/advanced_operations/package_delivery_drone). The local prototype does not dispatch, simulate or control aircraft. Its functioning delivery mode is employee ground delivery.

## Source basis and required professional review''')
p.write_text(s,encoding='utf-8')
p=Path('outputs/community-connect/README.md');s=p.read_text(encoding='utf-8-sig').replace('Manager: approve the application with a rationale.','Manager: release the employee for delivery duty with a staffing reason, then approve the application with a rationale.')
s+='''\n## Store manager authority\n\nOnly management can release an employee for delivery duty. Both acceptance and departure require that release. Employees cannot release themselves. Recalling an employee blocks new acceptance/departure, while a delivery already in progress can safely finish. Employee route entries do not override this staffing decision.\n\n## Drone roadmap\n\nThe architecture plan includes future central drone hubs, with Abingdon as an illustrative location for feasibility review. No aircraft connection, live mission simulation or flight control is implemented. Employee ground delivery remains the functional prototype mode.\n''';p.write_text(s,encoding='utf-8')
p=Path('work/build-brochure.py');s=p.read_text(encoding='utf-8-sig').replace('Choose whether to accept an available paid assignment.','The store manager decides who can be spared. Released employees choose whether to accept a paid assignment.').replace('A measurable decision','Longer-term delivery vision').replace('Review on-time delivery, completion, actual cost, assisted households served and employee feedback. Pause for serious safety issues, privacy incidents or unexplained ledger differences.','A future network could use central drone hubs, with Abingdon as a feasibility example. This requires a separate operator, approvals and business case. Ground delivery remains the prototype and fallback.');p.write_text(s,encoding='utf-8')
